(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["1-toledo-xi"]=[["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p1","Beginning of the Eleventh Toledan Council","VI. That priests ought not to inflict mutilations of limbs upon servants in any church, nor judge anything which is to be punished by death.",""],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p2","Beginning of the Eleventh Toledan Council","VII. What discretion ought to be in the rectors of churches, lest through unseemly discipline they incur the note of homicide.",""],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p3","VIII. That nothing of reward be received for the divine sacraments.","IX. What ought to be guarded, if anyone becomes a bishop through reward, or by what sentence he is to be struck, who after having received honor is discovered to have been ordained through reward.",""],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p4","VIII. That nothing of reward be received for the divine sacraments.","X. That all pontiffs and rectors of churches, at the time when they are to be ordained, promise under caution that they ought to live most justly.",""],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p5","VIII. That nothing of reward be received for the divine sacraments.","XI. Concerning the elucidation of an ancient canon, by which it is commanded that, if anyone has not taken the Eucharist received from a priest, he is to be driven out as sacrilegious.",""],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p6","VIII. That nothing of reward be received for the divine sacraments.","XII. That a penitent in the confines of death not be long suspended from reconciliation, and that the oblation of him who, being penitent yet not reconciled, has departed from this life, be received by the church.","NE TUMULTU CONCILIUM AGITETUR. NON DEBERE METROPOLITANUM A CONFINITIMORUM INSTRUCTIONE CESSARE. UT IN UNA PROVINCIA DIVERSITAS OFFICIORUM NON TENEATUR. DE DISCORDIA SACERDOTUM. DE COMPESCENDIS EXCESSIBUS SACERDOTUM. NON DEBERE SACERDOTIBUS QUALIBET IN ECCLESIA FAMILIS TRUNCATIONES MEMBRORUM FACERE NEC ALIQUID, QUOD MORTE PLECTENDUM EST, IUDICARE. QUAE DEBEAT DISCRETIO ECCLESIARUM RECTORIBUS ESSE, NE PER INCONDITAM DISCIPLINAM SUBEANT HOMICIDII NOTAM. NE QUICQUAM PRAEMII PRO DIVINIS SACRAMENTIS ACCIPIATUR. QUID CUSTODIRI DEBEAT, SI PER PRAEMIUM QUIS EPISCOPUS FIAT VEL QUA SENTENTIA FERIATUR, QUI POST HONOREM ACCEPTUM PER PRAEMIUM ORDINATUS FUISSE DETEGITUR. UT OMNES PONTIFICES RECTORESQUE ECCLESIARUM TEMPORE, QUO ORDINANDI SUNT, SUB CAUTIONE PROMITTANT, QUAM IUSTISSIME VIVERE DEBEANT. DE ELUCIDATIONE ANTIQUI CANONIS, QUO PRAECIPITUR, UT, SI QUIS ACCEPTAM A SACERDOTE EUCHARISTIAM NON SUMPSERIT, VELUT SACRILEGUS PROPELLATUR. NE IN CONFINIO MORTIS POENITENS A RECONCILIATIONE DIUTINE SUSPENDATUR, ET UT OBLATIO EIUS, QUI POENITENS NEC TAMEN RECONCILIATUS DE HAC VITA EXIERIT, AB ECCLESIA RECEPTETUR. DE SACERDOTIBUS, QUI VEXATI CADERE VIDENTUR. UT HI, QUI DOMINO CANUNT ATQUE SACRIFICANT, POST SE SEMPER HABEANT ADIUTORIA CONSTITUTA. DE INSTITUTIONE CERTI TEMPORIS, QUO CONCILIUM AGATUR. DE RELATIONE GRATIARUM PRO CONSUMMATIONE CONCILII."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p7","XIII. Concerning priests who, being vexed, are seen to fall.","IN THE NAME OF THE LORD BEGIN THE SYNODAL ACTS OF THE ELEVENTH TOLEDAN COUNCIL, HELD IN THE ROYAL CITY IN THE TOLEDAN SEE BY SEVENTEEN BISHOPS IN THE FOURTH YEAR OF THE REIGN OF THE GLORIOUS PRINCE WAMBA ON THE 7TH DAY OF THE IDES OF NOVEMBER, IN THE ERA 713.","IN NOMINE DOMINI INCIPIUNT GESTA SYNODALIA CONCILII TOLETANI XI ACTA IN URBE REGIA IN TOLEDANA SEDE A DECIM ET SEPTEM EPISCOPIS ANNO IIII REGNI GLORIOSI PRINCIPIS WAMBANI SUB DIE VII ID. NOVEMBR. ERA DCCXIII."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p8","XIII. Concerning priests who, being vexed, are seen to fall.","In the name of the holy Trinity, with the priests of the Carthaginian province gathered together in the Toledan city in the see of the blessed mother of the Lord, the Virgin Mary, in the fourth year of the most excellent and religious prince Wamba, on the 7th day of the Ides of November, the day of votive joy and the day of most desired thanksgiving occurred, in which it has been given to us both to see together and to weep over what tears we ought to the Lord concerning past things. For we were hitherto unstable on account of the corruption of a declining age, because the long series of times, the light of councils having been withdrawn, not so much vices were present as the mother of all errors, ignorance, it was injecting to idle minds. For to those seeing how the boiling pot of Babylonian confusion now turned away the times of councils, now ensnared the priests of the Lord from relaxed morals, for they were following the invitations of a purple prostitute, because the discipline of ecclesiastical assembly was not present, nor was there anyone who, the simple collation of words rather than the instruction of condensed reading being related, might render us more able to understand the exercise of those erring, because indeed the mystery of so great a matter befits the priests of God to know in such a way that they be not carried away unknown by the surface of words, but be found instructed in the sense of sound understanding, so that in discussing especially the secrets of this holy Trinity, they be made savory by manifestness rather than by eloquence. For thus, driven by the gift of the divine gift, according to the vow, the promise of eternal definition might be shown in the work. Whence what on the first day the tongue of the presiding metropolitan poured forth, on the third day the voice of all of us, collected individually, repeated. Therefore this is the tenor of our faith, which both from the head abundantly flowed forth and, having been approved by the members, gloriously shone forth:","In nomine sanctae trinitatis collectis in unum Carthaginis provinciae sacerdotibus in Toletana urbe in beatae matris domini Mariae virginis sede anno quarto excellentissimi ac religiosi Wambani principis sub die VII Id. Nov. res votivi gaudii et dies nimium optatae gratulationis occurrit, in quo nobis datum est conspicere pariter et deflere, quid lacrimarum de praeteritis domino debeamus. Eramus enim hucusque prolabentis saeculi colluvione instabiles, quia annosa series temporum subtracta luce conciliorum non tam vitia aderat quam matrem omnium errorum ignorantiam otiosis mentibus ingerebat. Cernentibus enim quomodo Babylonicae confusionis olla succensa nunc tempora conciliorum averteret, nunc sacerdotes domini de resolutis moribus irretiret, purpuratae enim meretricis sequebantur invitamenta, quia ecclesiastici conventus non aderat disciplina, nec erat, qui errantium exercitationem magis nos ad intellegendum redderet verborum simplex collatio quam relata condensae lectionis instructio, quia et revera tantae rei mysterium ita sacerdotes dei convenit nosse, ut non superficie verborum efferantur incogniti, sed sensus sanae intellegentiae repperiantur instructi, ut in disserendo praecipue huius sanctae trinitatis arcana plus evidentia quam eloquentia eos efficiat saporatos. Sic enim et divini muneris dono adacti, ut iuxta votum definitionis aeternae promissio monstraretur in opere. Unde quod primo die praesidentis metropolitani lingua praefudit, die tertia omnium nostrum vox singillatim collativa repetiit. Iste ergo est tenor fidei nostrae, qui et a capite copiose profluxit et a membris probatus gloriose emicuit:"],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p9","The Confession of Faith","We confess and believe the holy and ineffable Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit, one God naturally to be, of one substance, of one nature, of one also majesty and virtue; and the Father indeed not begotten, not created, but unbegotten we profess. For He Himself from no one draws origin, from whom both the Son received nativity and the Holy Spirit procession. Therefore He Himself is the fountain and origin of the whole divinity. The Father Himself also of ineffable substance begot the Son ineffably from His own substance, nor yet did He beget something other than what He Himself is: God of God, light of light. From Him therefore is all paternity in heaven and on earth. The Son also from the substance of the Father without beginning, before the ages born, yet not made, we confess, because neither was the Father without the Son, nor did the Son ever exist without the Father, and yet not as the Son from the Father, so the Father from the Son, because not the Father from the Son, but the Son from the Father received generation. The Son therefore is God from the Father, the Father however is God, but not from the Son. He, however, is the Son of the Father and God from the Father, equal yet in all things the Son to God the Father, because He neither began to be born at any time nor ceased. He also is believed to be of one substance with the Father, on account of which He is also called homousion to the Father, that is, of the same substance with the Father. For homo in Greek means \"one,\" usia however means \"substance,\" which, both being joined, sounds \"one substance.\" For neither from nothing nor from any other substance, but from the womb of the Father, that is from His substance, is the same Son to be believed to have been begotten or born, the Son also sempiternal. For if the Father was always, He always had a Son of whom He would be Father; and on account of this we confess the Son born of the Father without beginning, nor do we, because He is begotten of the Father, name Him a portion of a defective nature, but we assert that the perfect Father begot the perfect Son without diminution, without defect, because it is of divinity alone not to have an unequal Son. This Son of God also is Son by nature, not by choice, whom God the Father is to be believed to have begotten neither by will nor by necessity, because neither does any necessity fall upon God nor does will precede wisdom. The Holy Spirit also, who is the third person in the Trinity, we believe to be one and equal with God the Father and the Son, God of one substance, and also of one nature, not however begotten or created, but proceeding from both, to be the Spirit of both. This Holy Spirit also is believed neither unbegotten nor begotten, lest, if we should say \"unbegotten,\" we might speak of two fathers, or if \"begotten,\" we might be shown to present two sons; who yet is called not of the Father only nor of the Son only, but at the same time of the Father and of the Son. For He does not proceed from the Father into the Son, nor does He proceed from the Son to sanctify the creature, but He is shown to have proceeded from both at the same time, because He is acknowledged to be the charity or holiness of both. Therefore this Holy Spirit, sent from both, just as the Son is believed, yet is not held to be less than the Father and the Son, just as the Son on account of the flesh assumed testifies that He is less than the Father and the Holy Spirit. This is the related account of the holy Trinity, which ought neither to be called nor believed triple, but Trinity. Nor can it be rightly said that in one God there is a Trinity, but the one God is the Trinity. But in the relative names of the persons, the Father is referred to the Son, the Son to the Father, the Holy Spirit to both; which, when they are called three relative persons, yet one nature or substance is believed; nor, as we speak of three persons, do we preach three substances, but one substance, three however persons. For what the Father is, is not in reference to Himself, but to the Son; and what the Son is, is not in reference to Himself, but to the Father; similarly also the Holy Spirit is referred not to Himself, but to the Father and the Son relatively, in that He is preached as the Spirit of the Father and the Son. Likewise when we say \"God,\" it is not said in reference to something, as the Father to the Son or the Son to the Father or the Holy Spirit to the Father and the Son, but it is said of Himself specially, \"God.\" For if we are asked concerning each person, we must confess God. Therefore God the Father, God the Son, God the Holy Spirit is said singly, nor yet three gods, but one God. Likewise also the Father is omnipotent, and the Son is omnipotent, and the Holy Spirit is omnipotent, said singly, nor yet three omnipotents, but one omnipotent, just as also one light and one principle is preached. Singly therefore also each person is confessed and believed one God, one and undivided and equal deity, majesty or power is to them, nor is it diminished in the individuals, nor increased in the three, because neither does it have anything less, when each person is singly called God, nor more, when all three persons are pronounced one God. Therefore this holy Trinity, which is one and true God, neither recedes from number nor is contained by number. For in the relation of persons, number is discerned; in the substance of divinity however, what is enumerated is not comprehended. Therefore this alone intimates number, that they are in relation to one another, and they lack number in that which they are in themselves. For thus to this holy Trinity one natural name befits, so that it cannot be plural for the three persons. On account of this we believe that which is said in the sacred scriptures: \"Great is our Lord, and great is His virtue, and of His wisdom there is no number.\" Nor because we have said these three persons to be one God, can we say that the same is the Father whom the Son is, or that he is the Son who is the Father, or that he is the Holy Spirit who is the Father or the Son. For not the Father himself is the Son, nor the Holy Spirit himself is he who is the Father or the Son, yet he himself is the Father that which the Son is, he himself the Son that which the Father is, he himself the Father and the Son that which the Holy Spirit is, that is, one God in nature. For when we say \"he himself is not the Father whom the Son is,\" it refers to personal distinction; when however we say \"he himself is the Father that which the Son is, and he himself the Son that which the Father is, and he himself the Holy Spirit that which the Father and the Son are,\" it is shown to pertain to the nature by which He is God or the substance, because in substance they are one; for we distinguish the persons, we do not separate the deity. Therefore we acknowledge the Trinity in distinction of persons, we profess power on account of nature or substance. Therefore these three are one, namely in nature, not in person. Nor yet are these three persons to be esteemed separable, since none before another, none without another, is believed ever to have existed or to have done anything. For they are found inseparable both in that which they are and in that which they do, because between the Father begetting and the Son begotten or the Holy Spirit proceeding we believe there was no interval of time by which either the begetter would ever precede the begotten, or the begotten would be lacking to the begetter, or the proceeding Spirit would appear later to the Father or the Son. On account of this this inseparable and unconfused Trinity is both believed and preached by us. Therefore these three persons are called, according to what the greater ones define, so that they may be acknowledged. For if we attend to that which holy scripture says of wisdom: \"She is the brightness of eternal light,\" just as we see brightness to inhere inseparably to light, so we confess that the Son cannot be separated from the Father. Therefore those three persons of one and inseparable nature, just as we do not confuse, so we by no means preach them separable. Since indeed the Trinity itself evidently shows this dignity to us so that even in all things in which He wished, the persons are singly known. He does not permit one to be understood without the other, for neither is the Father known without the Son, nor is the Son found without the Father. For the very relation of the personal term forbids the persons to be separated, which, even when it does not name them simultaneously, intimates them simultaneously. But no one is able to hear each one of those names without being compelled to understand the other also. Since therefore these three are one and in one three, yet to each person remains his own property. For the Father has eternity without nativity, the Son has eternity with nativity, the Holy Spirit truly has procession without nativity. Concerning these three persons, we believe that the person of the Son alone for the liberation of the human race assumed true man without sin from the holy and immaculate Virgin Mary, from whom by a new order and by a new nativity He was born: by a new order, because invisible in divinity He might be shown visible in flesh; by a new nativity, however, He was born, because untouched virginity both knew not male intercourse and, having been made fecund by the Holy Spirit, ministered the material of flesh. Which birth of the virgin is grasped neither by reason nor by example; it will be singular. Nor yet is the Holy Spirit to be believed to be the father of the Son on account of the fact that Mary conceived, the same Holy Spirit overshadowing, lest we seem to assert two fathers of the Son, which indeed is impious to be said. In which wonderful conception, wisdom building itself a house, the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us. Nor yet was the Word itself so converted and changed into flesh that He ceased to be God who had wished to be man, so that not only the Word of God and the soul of man is there, and this whole is called both God on account of God and man on account of man. In which Son of God we believe there are two natures, one of divinity, the other of humanity, which the unity of person so joined in itself that neither can divinity ever be separated from humanity nor humanity from divinity. Whence perfect God, perfect also man in the unity of person. Nor yet, because we have said there are two natures in the Son, will we be accused of there being two persons in Him, lest, which may God forbid, a quaternity be seen to happen to the Trinity. For God the Word did not assume the person of man, but the nature, and in eternity the person of divinity received the temporal substance of flesh. Likewise we believe that God the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are of one substance, yet we do not say that the Virgin Mary bore the unity of this Trinity, but only the Son, who alone assumed our nature in the unity of His person. The incarnation also of this Son of God is to be believed to have been wrought by the whole Trinity, because the works of the Trinity are inseparable. Yet the Son alone received the form of a servant in the singularity of the person, not in the unity of the divine nature, in that which is proper to the Son, not that which is common to the Trinity. Which form was fitted to Him into the unity of the person, that the Son of God and the Son of man might be one Christ. Likewise the same Christ in these two natures consists of three substances: of the Word, which is to be referred to the essence of God alone; of the body and of the soul, which pertains to true man. He has therefore in Himself a twofold substance: of His divinity and of our humanity. Yet He, through that which from God the Father without beginning He came forth, He was only begotten, for He is received neither as made nor as predestined; through that however which He was born of the Virgin Mary, He is to be believed both born and made and predestined. Both those generations however are wonderful, because both from the Father before the ages He was begotten without a mother, and in the end of the ages from a mother He was begotten without a father. Who yet, according to that which He was created as man, is the Son of Mary His mother. Likewise, through that which He is God, He is equal to the Father; through that which He is man, He is less than the Father. Likewise He is to be believed greater and less than Himself. For in the form of God, the Son Himself is greater than Himself on account of the humanity assumed, by which divinity is greater. In the form of a servant however He is less than Himself, that is, in humanity, which is held to be less than divinity. For just as through the flesh assumed He is held to be less not only than the Father but than Himself, so according to the divinity, by which He is equal to the Father, both He Himself and the Father are greater than man, whom the person of the Son alone assumed. Likewise concerning that which is asked, whether the Son can be thus equal and less to the Holy Spirit, just as He is now believed equal, now less to the Father, we respond: According to the form of God, He is equal both to the Father and to the Holy Spirit; according to the form of a servant, He is less both than the Father and than the Holy Spirit, because neither did the Holy Spirit nor did God the Father, but the person of the Son alone, receive the flesh, through which He is believed to be less than those two persons. Likewise this Son is believed to be separated in person from God the Father and the Holy Spirit inseparably, from the assumed man however by nature; likewise, with man He exists as one person, with the Father however and the Holy Spirit by the nature of His divinity. The Son yet is to be believed to have been sent not only by the Father, but also by the Holy Spirit, in that which is said through the prophet: \"And now the Lord has sent me and His Spirit.\" By Himself also He is held to have been sent on account of that which the operation, not only of will, but of the whole Trinity, is acknowledged to be inseparable. For hence He who before the ages was called only-begotten, in time was made firstborn: only-begotten on account of the substance of the deity, firstborn on account of the assumed flesh. In which He received the form of man; according to evangelical truth, He is believed to have died without sin, who alone for us sustained that passion for our sins, His divinity yet being preserved, and, having been adjudged to death, received the true death of the flesh on the cross, and on the third day, having been raised by His own power, He rose from the tomb. By this example therefore of our head we confess the true resurrection of the flesh of all the dead, nor that we are to rise, as some deliriously believe, in air or in any other flesh, but in this in which we live, stand and are moved. The example of this holy resurrection having been completed, the same our Lord and Saviour, ascending, returned to the paternal seat, from which He never departed through divinity, sitting there at the right hand of the Father, He is awaited at the end of the ages, the Judge of all the living and the dead. From there with all the saints He will come to bear judgment, to render to each the debt of his own reward, just as each, placed in the body, has done, whether good or evil. The catholic church indeed, purchased with precious blood, we believe with Him will reign for ever, within whose bosom placed, we believe and confess one baptism for the remission of all sins, under which faith we truly believe the resurrection of the dead and await the joys of the future age. This only is to be prayed and sought by us, that when, judgment having been completed and finished, the Son shall have delivered the kingdom to God the Father, He may make us participants of His kingdom, so that through this faith, by which we have clung to Him, we may reign with Him without end.","Confitemur et credimus sanctam atque ineffabilem trinitatem, patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum, unum deum naturaliter esse, unius substantiae, unius naturae, unius quoque maiestatis atque virtutis; et patrem quidem non genitum, non creatum, sed ingenitum profitemur. Ipse enim a nullo originem ducit, ex quo et filius nativitatem et spiritus sanctus processionem accepit. Fons ergo ipse et origo est totius divinitatis. Ipse quoque pater essentia quidem ineffabilis substantiae suae filium ineffabiliter genuit, nec tamen aliud quam, quod ipse est, genuit, deus deum, lux lucem. Ab ipso ergo est omnis paternitas in caelo et in terra. Filium quoque de substantia patris sine initio ante saecula natum, nec tamen factum esse fatemur, quia nec pater sine filio, nec filius aliquando exstitit sine patre, et tamen non sicut filius de patre, ita pater de filio, quia non pater a filio, sed filius a patre generationem accepit, filius ergo deus de patre est, pater autem deus, sed non de filio, ille autem filius patris et deus de patre, aequalis tamen per omnia filius deo patri, quia nec nasci coepit aliquando nec desiit. Hic etiam unius cum patre substantiae creditur, propter quod et homousion patri dicitur, hoc est eiusdem cum patre substantiae. Omo enim graece unum, usia vero substantia dicitur, quod utrumque coniunctum sonat una substantia. Neque enim de nihilo neque de aliqua alia substantia, sed de patris utero, id est de substantia eius idem filius genitus vel natus esse credendus est, sempiternus et filius. Quod si semper pater fuit, semper habuit filium, cuius pater esset; et ob hoc filium de patre natum sine initio confitemur, nec enim eundem filium dei pro eo, quod de patre sit genitus, defectae naturae portiunculam nominamus, sed perfectum patrem, perfectum filium sine minutione, sine defectione genuisse asserimus, quia solius divinitatis est inaequalem filium non habere. Hic etiam filius dei natura est filius, non optione, quem deus pater nec voluntate nec necessitate genuisse credendus est, quia nec ulla in deo necessitas cadit nec voluntas sapientiam praevenit. Spiritum quoque sanctum, qui est tertia in trinitate persona, unum atque aequalem cum deo patre et filio credimus esse deum unius substantiae, unius quoque esse naturae, non tamen genitum vel creatum, sed ab utrisque procedentem amborum esse spiritum. Hic etiam spiritus sanctus nec ingenitus nec genitus creditur, ne aut, si ingenitum dixerimus, duos patres dicamus, aut si genitum, duos filios praedicare monstremur; qui tamen nec patris tantum nec filii tantum, sed simul patris et filii dicitur. Nec enim de patre procedit in filium vel de filio procedit ad sanctificandam creaturam, sed simul ab utrisque processisse monstratur, quia caritas sive sanctitas amborum esse agnoscitur. Hic igitur spiritus sanctus missus ab utrisque, sicut filius creditur, sed minor a patre et filio non habetur, sicut filius propter assumptam carnem minorem se patre et spiritu sancto esse testatur. Haec est sanctae trinitatis relata narratio, quae non triplex, sed trinitas et dici et credi debet. Nec recte dici potest, ut in uno deo sit trinitas, sed unus deus trinitas. In relativis vero personarum nominibus pater ad filium, filius ad patrem, spiritus sanctus ad utrosque refertur, quae cum relativae tres personae dicantur, una tamen natura vel substantia creditur; nec sicut tres personas, ita tres substantias praedicamus, sed unam substantiam, tres autem personas. Quod enim pater est, non ad se, sed ad filium, et quod filius est, non ad se, sed ad patrem est; similiter et spiritus sanctus non ad se, sed ad patrem et filium relative refertur in eo, quod spiritus patris et filii praedicatur. Item cum dicimus deus, non ad aliquid dicitur, sicut pater ad filium vel filius ad patrem vel spiritus sanctus ad patrem et filium, sed ad se specialiter dicitur deus. Nam si de singulis personis interrogemur, deum necesse est fateamur. Deus ergo pater, deus filius, deus spiritus sanctus singulariter dicitur, nec tamen tres dii, sed unus est deus. Item et pater omnipotens et filius omnipotens et spiritus sanctus omnipotens singulariter dicitur, nec tamen tres omnipotentes, sed unus omnipotens, sicut et unum lumen unumque principium praedicatur. Singulariter ergo et unaquaeque persona unus deus confitetur et creditur, una illi et indivisa atque aequalis deitas, maiestatis sive potestas, nec minoratur in singulis, nec augetur in tribus, quia nec minus aliquid habet, cum unaquaeque persona deus singulariter dicitur, nec amplius, cum totae tres personae unus deus enuntiatur. Haec ergo sancta trinitas, quae unus et verus est deus, nec recedit a numero nec capitur numero. In relatione enim personarum numerus cernitur, in divinitatis vero substantia, quid enumeratum sit, non compraehenditur. Ergo hoc solum numerum insinuant, quod ad invicem sunt, et in hoc numero carent, quod inse sunt. Nam ita huic sanctae trinitati unum naturale convenit nomen, ut tribus personis non possit esse plurale. Ob hoc ergo credimus illud in sacris litteris dictum: Magnus dominus noster et magna virtus eius et sapientiae eius non est numerus. Nec quia tres has personas esse diximus, unum deum, eundem esse patrem quem filium, vel eum esse filium, qui est pater, aut eum, qui est spiritus sanctus, vel patrem et filium dicere poterimus. Non enim ipse pater est filius nec spiritus sanctus ipse, qui est vel pater vel filius, cum tamen ipse est pater quod filius, ipse filius quod pater, ipse pater et filius quod spiritus sanctus, id est natura unus deus. Cum enim dicimus non ipsum esse patrem quem filium, ad personalem distinctionem refertur, cum autem dicamus ipsum esse patrem quod filium et ipsum filium quod patrem, ipsum spiritum sanctum quod patrem et filium, ad naturam, qua deus est vel substantiam pertinere monstratur, quia substantia unum sunt, personas enim distinguimus, non deitatem separamus. Trinitatem igitur in personarum distinctione agnoscimus, potestatem propter naturam vel substantiam profitemur. Tria ergo ista unum sunt, natura scilicet non persona. Nec tamen tres istae personae separabiles aestimandae sunt, cum nulla ante aliam, nulla sine alia vel exstitisse vel quippiam operasse aliquando creditur. Inseparabiles enim inveniuntur et in eo, quod sunt, et in eo, quod faciunt, quia inter generantem patrem et genitum filium vel procedentem spiritum sanctum nullum fuisse credimus temporis intervallum, quo aut genitor genitum aliquando praecederet aut genitus genitori deesset, aut procedens spiritus patri vel filio posterior appareret. Ob hoc ergo inseparabilis et inconfusa haec trinitas a nobis et creditur et praedicatur. Tres igitur personae istae dicuntur, iuxta quod maiores definiunt, ut agnoscantur. Nam si attendamus illud, quod scriptura sancta dicit de sapientia: Splendor est lucis aeternae, sicut splendorem lucis videmus inseparabiliter inhaerere, sic confitemur filium a patre separari non posse. Tres ergo illas unius atque inseparabilis naturae personas, sicut non confundimus, ita separabiles nulla tenus praedicamus. Quando quidem ita nobis hoc dignitatem ipsa trinitas evidenter ostendere, ut etiam in omnibus, quibus voluit, singillatim personae agnoscantur. Unam sine altera non permittat intellegi, nec enim pater absque filio cognoscitur, nec sine patre filius invenitur. Relatio quippe ipsa vocaboli personalis personas separari vetat, quae etiam non simul nominat, simul insinuat. Nemo autem audire potest unum quodque istorum nominum, in quo non intellegere cogatur et alterum. Cum igitur haec tria sint unum et in unum tria, est tamen unicuique personae manens sua proprietas. Pater enim aeternitatem habet sine nativitate, filius aeternitatem cum nativitate, spiritus vero sanctus processionem sine nativitate. De his tribus personis solam filii personam pro liberatione humani generis hominem verum sine peccato de sancta et immaculata Maria virgine credimus assumpsisse, de qua novo ordine novaque nativitate est genitus, novo ordine, quia invisibilis divinitate visibilis monstraretur in carne, nova autem nativitate est genitus, quia intacta virginitas et virilem coitum nescivit et fecundatam per spiritum sanctum carnis materiam ministravit. Qui partus virginis nec ratione colligitur nec exemplo; erit singulare. Nec tamen spiritus sanctus pater esse credendus est filii pro eo, quod Maria eodem sancto spiritu obumbrante concepit, ne duos patres filii videamur asserere, quod utique nefas est dici. In quo mirabili conceptu aedificans sibi sapientia domum, verbum caro factum est et habitavit in nobis. Nec tamen verbum ipsum ita in carnem conversum atque mutatum est, ut desisteret deus esse, qui homo esse voluisset, ut non tantum ibi sit verbum dei et hominis anima, atque hoc totum et deus dicatur propter deum et homo propter hominem. In quo dei filio duas credimus esse naturas, unam divinitatis, alteram humanitatis, quas sibi in se una Christi persona univit, ut nec divinitas ab humanitate nec humanitas a divinitate possit aliquando seiungi. Unde perfectus deus, perfectus et homo in unitate personae. Nec tamen, quia duas diximus in filio esse naturas, accusabimur in eo esse duas personas, ne trinitati, quod absit, accidere videatur quaternitas. Deus enim verbum non accepit personam hominis, sed naturam, et in aeternum persona divinitatis temporalem accepit substantiam carnis. Item unius substantiae credimus esse deum patrem et filium et spiritum sanctum, non tamen dicimus, ut huius trinitatis unitatem Maria virgo genuerit, sed tantum modo filium, qui solus naturam nostram in unitate personae suae assumpsit. Incarnationem quoque huius filii dei tota trinitas operata esse credenda est, quia inseparabilia sunt opera trinitatis. Solus tamen filius formam servi accepit in singularitate personae, non in unitate divinae naturae, in id, quod est proprium filii, non quod commune trinitati. Quae forma illi ad unitatem personae coaptata est, ut filius dei et filius hominis unus sit Christus. Item idem Christus in his duabus naturis tribus exstat substantiis, verbi, quod ad solius dei essentiam referendum est, corporis et animae, quod ad verum hominem pertinet. Habet igitur in se geminam substantiam divinitatis suae et humanitatis nostrae. Hic tamen per hoc, quod de deo patre sine initio prodiit, natus tantum, nam neque factus neque praedestinatus accipitur; per hoc tamen, quod de Maria virgine natus est, et natus et factus et praedestinatus esse credendus est. Ambae tamen illae generationes mirabiles, quia et de patre ante saecula sine matre est genitus et in fine saeculorum de matre sine patre est generatus. Qui tamen, secundum quod homo creatus est, Mariae matris est filius. Item, per hoc quod deus est, aequalis patri, per hoc quod homo, minor est patre. Item et maior et minor se ipso esse credendus est. Forma enim dei etiam ipse filius se ipso maior est propter humanitatem assumptam, qua divinitas maior est. In forma autem servi se ipso minor est, id est humanitate, quae minor divinitate accipitur. Nam sicut per assumptam carnem non tantum a patre, sed a seipso minor accipitur, ita secundum divinitatem, qua aequalis est patri, et ipse et pater maior est homine, quem sola filii persona assumpsit. Item in eo, quod quaeritur, utrum possit filius sic aequalis et minor esse spiritui sancto, sicut nunc aequalis, nunc minor patre creditur esse, respondemus: Secundum formam dei aequalis est et patri et spiritui sancto, secundum formam servi minor est et a patre et ab spiritu sancto, quia nec spiritus sanctus nec deus pater, sed sola filii persona suscepit carnem, per quam minor esse creditur illis personis duabus. Item hic filius a deo patre et sancto spiritu inseparabiliter discretus creditur esse persona, ab homine autem assumpto natura; item, cum homine unus exstat persona, cum patre vero et spiritu sancto natura divinitatis suae. Filius tamen non solum a patre, sed a spiritu sancto missus esse credendus est in eo, quod per prophetam dicitur: Et nunc dominus misit me et spiritus eius. A se ipso quoque missus accipitur pro eo, quod inseparabilis non solum voluntatis, sed operatio totius trinitatis agnoscitur. Hinc enim, qui ante saecula unigenitus est vocatus, temporaliter primogenitus factus est, unigenitus propter deitatis substantiam, primogenitus propter assumptae carnis formam, in qua suscepit hominis formam; iuxta evangelicam veritatem sine peccato mortuus creditur, qui solus pro nobis passionem ipsam, salva tamen divinitate sua, pro delictis nostris sustinuit, mortique adiudicatus et cruci veram carnis mortem excepit, tertio quoque die virtute propria suscitatus a sepulchro surrexit. Hoc ergo exemplo capitis nostri confitemur veram fidem resurrectionis carnis omnium mortuorum, nec in aere vel qualibet alia carne, ut quidam delirantes surrecturos nos credunt, sed in ista, qua vivimus, consistimus et movemur. Peracto huius sanctae resurrectionis exemplo, idem dominus noster atque salvator paternam ascendendo sedem repetiit, de qua numquam per divinitatem discessit, illic ad dexteram patris sedens, exspectatur in fine saeculorum, iudex omnium vivorum et mortuorum. Inde cum sanctis omnibus veniet ad ferendum iudicium, reddere unicuique mercedis propriae debitum, prout quisque gesserit in corpore positus sive bonum sive malum. Ecclesiam sane catholicam pretioso sanguine comparatam cum eo credimus in perpetuum regnaturam, intra cuius gremium constituti unum baptisma credimus et confitemur remissionem omnium peccatorum, sub qua fide et resurrectionem mortuorum veraciter credimus et futuri saeculi gaudia exspectamus. Hoc tantum orandum nobis est et petendum, ut cum peracto finitoque iudicio tradiderit filius regnum deo patri, participes nos efficiat regni sui, ut per hanc fidem, qua illi inhaesimus, cum illo sine fine regnemus."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p10","The Confession of Faith","This is the faith of our confession set forth, through which the dogma of all heretics is destroyed, through which the hearts of the faithful are cleansed, through which also one approaches gloriously to God; by its sacrosanct savor, under a three-day fast, with the collation of continual recitations dripping, we passed on to those things which are subjoined, to be decreed on the following day.","Haec est confessionis nostrae fides exposita, per quam omnium hereticorum dogma perimitur, per quam fidelium corda mundantur, per quam etiam ad deum gloriose acceditur, cuius sacrosancto sapore sub triduano ieiunio continua relationum collatione rorantes ad ea, quae subnexa sunt, sequenti die decernenda transivimus."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p11","Canon I","The lord priests, sitting in a place of blessing, ought not to make a din with indiscreet voices nor to be disturbed by any tumults, nor also to be agitated by any vain fables or laughter, and, which is worse, to pour forth tumultuous voices with obstinate disputations. For if anyone, as the apostle says, thinks himself to be religious, not restraining his tongue but deceiving his own heart, this one's religion is vain. For justice loses its cult when the silences of judgment are confused by a crowd of brawlers, the prophet saying: \"The cult of justice shall be silence.\" Therefore, whatever is done either in the consultations of those sitting together or is proposed on the part of those pleading, ought so to be brought forth with the most mild relation of words that neither the sense of the hearers be disturbed by contentious voices nor the vigor of the judges be enervated by tumult. Whoever therefore in the assembly of the council shall have believed these things which are promised to be violent and, against these interdicts, shall have disturbed the council either by tumult or by contumelies or by laughter, according to the edict of the divine law, which commands: \"Cast out the scorner, and with him will go forth strife,\" let him, with all dishonor, drawn away from the assembly, depart from the common gathering and undergo the sentence of excommunication for three days.","In loco benedictionis consedentes domini sacerdotes nullis debent aut indiscretis vocibus perstrepere aut quibuslibet tumultibus perturbari, nullis etiam vanis fabulis vel risibus agi et, quod est deterius, obstinatis disceptationibus tumultuosas voces effundere. Si quis enim, ut apostolus ait, putat se religiosum esse non refrenans linguam suam, sed seducens cor suum, huius vana est religio. Cultum enim suum iustitia perdit, quando silentia iudicii obstrepentium turba confundit, dicente propheta: Erit cultus iustitiae silentium. Debet ergo, quicquid aut consedentium consultationibus agitur aut a causantium parte proponitur, sic mitissima verborum relatione proferri, ut nec contentiosis vocibus sensus audientium turbent nec iudicum vigorem de tumultu enervent. Quicumque ergo in conventu concilii haec, quae promissa sunt violenta crediderit et contra haec interdicta aut tumultu aut contumeliis vel risibus concilium perturbaverit, iuxta divinae legis edictum, quae praecipitur: Eice derisorem et exibit cum eo iurgium, cum omni dedecore de consessione abstractus a communi coetu secedat et trium dierum excommunicationis sententiam perferat."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p12","Canon II","By as much as anyone obtains the place of a very high summit, by so much it is necessary that he excels others in the grace of merits, so that in that in which he presides over individuals, he may singularly adorn the eminence of sanctity, always having both in his mouth the sword of truth and in his work the efficacy of light, and according to Paul, be able to exhort in sound doctrine and to refute those contradicting. Therefore we ought greatly to consider the grade of our order or the measure of governance received, so that we who have received the office of preaching be not deprived of divine reading by any cares. For the minds of certain pontiffs are so excluded from the grace of reading by the idleness of the body, that a mute herald does not find who may offer doctrine to the subject flocks. Therefore always it will be insisted by the greater ones that they not suffer those whom they guard under the care of their governance to perish by hunger for the word of God. Just as it is to be watched by metropolitans over those bordering and the rest devoted to ecclesiastical orders, so it is to be watched by those bordering over the number of religious committed to them, in what manner the ignorance of such persons may be imbued with the eruditions of the divine law, so that a prelate, unceasingly seeking his subjects with solicitude, may either joyfully acknowledge their progress or instruct their ignorance without arrogance. Therefore it has pleased concerning such persons according to the institutes of the Toledan council to define this specially: that either they spontaneously take up the necessary intention of thoroughly learning, or by the greater ones they be compelled unwillingly to the exercises of reading.","Quantum quis praecelsi culminis obtinet locum, tantum necesse est praecedat ceteros gratia meritorum, ut in eo, quod praesidet singulis, singulariter ornet eminentiam sanctitatis habens semper et in ore gladium veritatis et in opere efficientiam luminis et iuxta Paulum potens sit exhortari in doctrina sana et contradicentes revincere. Nos proinde nostri ordinis gradum vel suscepti regiminis modum magnopere cogitare debemus, ut, qui officium praedicationis suscepimus, nullis curis a divina lectione privemur. Nam quorundam mentes pontificum ita corporis otio a lectionis gratia secluduntur, ut qui doctrinam subditis gregibus exhibeat, non inveniat, praeco mutus. Insistendum ergo semper erit maioribus, ut, quos sub regiminis sui cura tuentur, fame verbi dei perire non sinant. Sicut metropolitanis in confinitimos ceterosque ecclesiasticis ordinibus deditos, sic confinitimis in commisso sibi religiosorum numero vigilandum est, qualiter nescientia talium divinae legis eruditionibus imbuatur, ita indesinenter sollicitudine praelatus quisque subditos quaerens aut profectum eorum laetabundus agnoscat aut nescientiam sine arrogantia instruat. Placuit ergo de talibus iuxta instituta Toletani concilii hoc specialiter definire, ut aut sponte sumant intentionem necessariam perdiscendi aut a maioribus ad lectionis exercitia cogantur inviti."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p13","Canon III","Concerning those who are carried about by every wind of doctrine against the will of the apostle, it has pleased this holy council that, compelled by the authority of the metropolitan see, the citizens and rectors of churches of each province hold one and the same manner in psalm-singing which they shall have known to be instituted in the metropolitan see, nor suffer themselves to be disjoined from the metropolitan see by any diversity of any order or office. For thus it is just, that each one take the rules of magisterium from that place from which he receives the consecration of honor, so that according to the decrees of the greater ones, the see which is the mother of sacerdotal dignity to each one may be the mistress of ecclesiastical practice. But to abbots, for the offices permitted to them, which are to be fulfilled by them regularly according to the will of their bishop, it is not lawful to celebrate the other public offices, that is, vespers, matins or mass, otherwise than in the principal church. Whoever moreover shall have been a violator of these decrees, let him, deprived of communion for six months, remain with the metropolitan to be corrected under the censure of penance, so that before him he may both wash away the guilt of past transgression with tears and studiously learn the necessary doctrine of offices. Under this rule of discipline, therefore, let the metropolitan not only bind the pontiffs or priests of his whole province, but also let the other bishops compel the rectors of churches subject to them to obey these institutions.","De his, qui contra apostoli voluntatem circumferuntur omni vento doctrinae, placuit hoc sancto concilio, ut metropolitanae sedis auctoritate coacti uniuscuiusque provinciae cives rectoresque ecclesiarum in unum eundemque in psallendo teneant modum, quem in metropolitana sede cognoverit institutum, nec aliqua diversitate cuiusque ordinis vel officii a metropolitana se patiantur sede disiungi. Sic enim iustum est, ut inde unusquisque sumat regulas magisterii, unde honoris consecrationem accipit, ut iuxta maiorum decreta sedes, quae unicuique sacerdotalis mater est dignitatis, sit ecclesiasticae magistra rationis. Abbatibus sane indultis officiis, quae iuxta voluntatem sui episcopi regulariter illis implenda sunt, cetera officia publica, id est vesperam, matutinum sive missam aliter quam in principali ecclesia celebrare non liceat. Quisquis autem horum decretorum violator exstiterit, sex mensibus communione privatus apud metropolitanum sub poenitentiae censura permaneat corrigendus, qualiter apud illum et praeteritae transgressionis culpam lacrimis diluat et necessariam officiorum doctrinam studiose addiscat. Sub ista ergo regula disciplinae non solum metropolitanus totius suae provinciae pontifices vel sacerdotes adstringat, sed etiam ceteri episcopi subiectos sibi ecclesiarum rectores obtemperare institutionibus cogant."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p14","Canon IV","Just as everyone who loves his brother is of God, so everyone who hates his neighbor is of the devil. For by love alone is it discerned of what origin each one is approved to be born, John saying: \"In this the children of God and the children of the devil are manifest: Everyone who does not do justice is not of God, and he who does not love his brother, because this is the announcement which you have heard from the beginning, that we love one another.\" And after a little: \"Everyone who hates his brother is a murderer, and you know that every murderer does not have eternal life abiding in himself.\" Behold, he is probably declared a murderer who is divided from fraternal society. For even if his hand is not poised to kill, yet on account of that, because he is cruel for harming, he is already held a murderer by God. That one lives, and this one is convicted to be already a slayer. Since therefore to these precepts the blessed apostle Paul agrees with consonant preaching, saying: \"Let not the sun go down upon your wrath, and give no place to the devil.\" The persons of certain priests have been related to us to have grown so fierce in great obstinacy of discord, that not only does the setting of the sun not recall them from wrath, but nor does even a long lapse of times bend them to the good of charity, indeed in whose hearts Christ, the sun of justice, has set, so that they can scarcely return to the light of charity. Therefore the oblations of these and of similar discordant brothers we decree by no means to be received, according to the definition of the ancient canon. To the persons however of the discordant ones we command by a special definition, that before true reconciliation joins them, let none of them dare to approach the altar of the Lord or receive the grace of holy communion, but they shall compensate with double time through penance for that which they have served discord. But if one of them, the other despising, shall have run to the satisfaction of charity, from that time let him be received as a peaceable person within the church from which he is proved to have hastened to concord, the superior sentence yet being preserved, that he pay double in the satisfaction of penance the time which he expended in wrath.","Sicut omnis, qui diligit fratrem suum, ex deo, ita omnis, qui odit proximum, ex diabolo est. Dilectione enim sola discernitur quis, ex quo genitus approbetur, dicente Ioanne: In hoc manifesti sunt filii dei et filii diaboli: Omnis, qui non facit iustitiam, non est de deo, et qui non diligit fratrem suum, quoniam haec est annuntiatio, quam ab initio audistis, ut diligamus alterutrum. Et post paululum: Omnis, qui odit fratrem, homicida est, et scitis, quoniam omnis homicida non habet vitam aeternam in se manentem. Ecce homicida esse probabiliter declaratur, qui a fraterna societate dividitur. Nam etsi manus non maneat ad occidendum, pro eo tamen, quia immitis est ad nocendum, iam a deo homicida tenetur. Vivit ille, et iste iam interfector esse convincitur. Cum igitur his praeceptis beatus apostolus Paulus consona praedicatione concordet dicens: Non occidat sol super iracundiam vestram et nolite locum dare diabolo, relatae sunt nobis quorundam sacerdotum personae in tantam obstinationis efferbuisse discordiam, ut non solum illos ab ira occasus solis non revocet, sed nec annosa quidem transactio temporum ad bonum caritatis reclinet, quippe in quorum cordibus sol iustitiae Christus occubuit, ad lumen caritatis redire vix possint. Horum igitur et similium discordantium fratrum oblationes iuxta antiqui canonis definitionem nullo modo recipiendas esse censemus. Personis tamen discordantium id speciali definitione praecipimus, ut, antequam eos reconciliatio vera innectat, nullus eorum accedere ad altare domini audeat vel gratiam communionis sanctae percipiat, sed geminato tempore per poenitentiam compensabunt, quo discordiae servierunt. Quod si unus eorum alio contemnente ad satisfactionem caritatis cucurrerit, ex eo tempore ut pacificus intra ecclesiam receptetur, ex quo ad concordiam festinasse convincitur, sententia tamen superiori servata, ut tempus, quod in iram expendit, geminatum in poenitentiae satisfactione persolvat."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p15","Canon V","No one ought to be disturbed by the excesses of the life of prelates, because it is very unworthy that those who are called thrones of God be disturbed by a light movement, and who ought to be the way of justice, themselves become a seedbed of strife and rapine. How therefore according to the apostle will such persons be irreprehensible, who not only do reprehensible things, but rather commit deadly and execrable things? For certain priests have been related to us, that, with all gravity of the priestly order omitted, they anticipate the judgment of those hearing with fury and refuse to hear of the excesses of dishonest movement, for which it would have been fitting for them to sustain the judgments of equity. For while they flatter themselves concerning the summit of their honor, they refuse to have patience, and those who ought to have preserved the state of unshaken truth suddenly change the purpose of religion and, with headlong fury, anticipate judgment, and thus in that in which the assembly of judgment had determined that they should sustain, by usurpation they act from which they may be confounded concerning presumptions. Who yet are to be punished either by loss together with excommunication, or, compositions of things having been omitted, they will be cured by the satisfaction of penance alone. For those who are supported by the resources of their own property, or who are known to have previously transferred their property in the name of the church in the presence of others, if either by themselves or through subjects or through any persons they plunder the things of others or do anything of presumption or slaughter, both in fiscal things and also in the dominion of any persons constituted, let them both repair things seized or presumed according to the laws of the most excellent prince, and for an excess contrary to religion, which they have wickedly perpetrated before judgment, let them be punished with excommunication of two weeks. And those who seem to have nothing of property are to be healed with great art of discretion, so that they may neither redeem illicit daring with the resources of churches, nor themselves altogether remain alien from punishment. For it is not just that for the evil acts of priests, the churches over which they preside should sustain loss, nor for such excesses should satisfaction be exacted from churches, since the church informs its cultivators not to strife but to honesty. Therefore it has pleased to define concerning such persons who, having no property of their own, shall have been usurpers in anything or shall have inflicted slaying or any presumption upon any persons, that they ought not, by any bending of their state, to be delivered to the servitude of men, but just as whoever that presumptuous person shall have been, thus also he shall be subject to the laws of penance. If he is proved to be a presumptor to the sum of ten solidi, let him be purified by the satisfaction of twenty days of penance, so that whether he has perpetrated an excess of a lesser or of a greater sum, he always recompenses the same with double satisfaction of penance. But the servants of churches who are known to have done such excesses, we remit to secular laws to be heard. And let these things suffice concerning general excess. But pursuing the order of speciality, if any bishop by any fraud or subtlety shall have defiled by adulterous pollution the wife, daughter, granddaughter, or any person pertaining to a magnate in whatever degree related to him, let him both lose the grade of his own honor and be bound under relegation of exile.","Nullus vita praesulum perturbari debet excessibus, quia valde indignum est, ut, qui throni dei vocantur, levi motione turbentur, et qui debent esse iter iustitiae, ipsi efficiantur seminarium litis atque rapinae. Quomodo ergo huiusmodi iuxta apostolum irreprehensibiles erunt, qui non solum reprehensibilia faciunt, sed mortifera potius et exsecranda committunt? Relati enim nobis sunt quidam ex sacerdotibus, quod omni gravitate sacerdotalis ordinis praetermissa audientium iudicium furore praeveniant et excessus inhonestae motionis audire refugiunt, pro quibus eos oportuerat aequitatis iudicia sustinere. Dum enim de honoris sui culmine blandiuntur, patientiam habere refugiunt, et qui inconcussae debuerant veritatis conservare statum, subito religionis mutant propositum et praecipiti furore iudicium antecedunt, sicque in quo decreverat eos iudicii sustinere conventum, pervasione agunt, unde de praesumptionibus confundantur. Qui tamen aut damno pariter et excommunicatione plectendi sunt aut omissis compositionibus rerum sola satisfactione poenitentiae curabuntur. Illi enim, qui rei propriae facultate suffulti sunt, aut, qui rem suam iam ante in nomine ecclesiae praesentes transfudisse noscuntur, si aut per se aut per subditos seu per quoslibet aliena diripiunt vel praesumptionis seu caedis quippiam agunt, tam in rebus fiscalibus quam etiam in quorumlibet dominio constitutis, et pervasa vel praesumpta de rebus iuxta leges excellentissimi principis sarciant, et pro excessu religioni contrario, quod inhoneste ante iudicium patraverunt, duarum hebdomadarum excommunicatione plectendi sunt. Et qui nihil proprietatis habere videntur, magna discretionis arte medendi sunt, quo nec ausus illicitos ecclesiarum facultatibus redimant, nec ipsi penitus extorres a poena persistant. Nec enim iustum est, ut pro pravis actibus sacerdotum ecclesiae, quibus praeminent, sustineant damnum aut pro excessibus talibus satisfactio ab ecclesiis exigatur, cum ecclesia cultores suos non ad litem, sed ad honestatem informet. De talibus ergo placuit definire, qui nullis habitis rebus propriis aut in quocumque pervasores exstiterint aut quibuslibet personis caedes vel quodcumque praesumptionis intulerint, nulla eos incurvatione status sui servituti hominum debere addici, sed iuxta quod praesumptuosus quisquis ille exstiterit, ita et poenitentiae legibus subiacebit, si in decem solidorum summam praesumptor esse convincitur, viginti dierum poenitentiae satisfactione purgetur, ita ut sive minoris sive maioris summae excessum peregerit, similiter geminata hoc semper satisfactione poenitentiae recompenset. Servos tamen ecclesiarum, qui huiusmodi excessus operasse noscuntur, ad leges saeculares audiendos remittimus. Et haec quidem de generali excessu dicta sufficiant. Ceterum specialitatis ordinem prosequentes, si quis episcoporum magnati cuiusquam uxorem, filiam, neptem seu quolibet illi gradu adhibito pertinentem quacumque fraude vel subtilitate adulterina pollutione fedaverit, et honoris proprii gradum amittat et sub exilii religatione teneatur."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p16","Canon VI","To those by whom the sacraments of the Lord are to be administered, it is not lawful to agitate the judgment of blood. And therefore it is to be greatly forbidden by such excesses, lest, agitated by the movements of indiscreet presumption, they either presume to judge by their own sentence that which is to be punished by death, or inflict mutilations upon any persons either by themselves or command them to be inflicted. But if anyone, unmindful of these precepts, shall have done such a thing either to the servants of his church or to any persons, let him both be deprived of the honor of his conceded order and be held bound in a place under perpetual damnation in a prison of relegation, to whom yet communion is not to be denied departing from this life on account of the mercy of the Lord, who does not wish the death of the sinner, but that he be converted and live.","His, a quibus domini sacramenta tractanda sunt, iudicium sanguinis agitare non licet. Et ideo magnopere talibus excessibus prohibendum est, ne indiscretae praesumptionis motibus agitati aut, quod morte plectendum est, sententia propria iudicare praesumant, aut truncationes quibuslibet personis aut per se inferant aut inferendas praecipiant. Quod si quisquam horum immemor praeceptorum aut in ecclesiae suae familiis aut in quibuslibet personis tale aliquid fecerit, et concessi ordinis honore privatus et loco sub perpetuo damnationis teneatur religatus ergastulo, cui tamen communio exeunti ex hac vita non neganda est propter domini misericordiam, qui non vult peccatoris mortem, sed ut convertatur et vivat."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p17","Canon VII","Since according to the edict of ancient institution, benevolence ought to act more than severity in correcting, more exhortation than commotion, more charity than power, it has been related to us that certain of the brethren, pursuing subjects more with the spite of hatred than with the zeal of correction, while they pretend to apply spiritual correction to them, suddenly bring indiscreet death, when they cast them out unheard and punish them with illicit judgments under penance. Therefore from now on let it not be free for perverse wills to pretend that which they feign, but as often as any of the subjects is to be corrected, either public discipline is to be cared for by the priest, or, if it pleases the rectors otherwise, the testimony of two or three spiritual brothers having been specially applied, both the measure of the crime and the measure of penance may be determined, yet in such a way that if he shall have known that he is worthy of exile or relegation, or what other particular judgment may have decreed, the measure of penance which the priest shall have imposed upon the transgressor before three brothers ought to be recorded with the subscription of the proper hand of him who pronounced the sentence. And thus it will come about that neither may transgressors incur the destruction of their life without testimony, nor may rectors blush that they are accused concerning the killings of any persons.","Cum iuxta antiquam institutionis edictum plus erga corrigendos agere debeat benevolentia quam severitas, plus cohortatio quam commotio, plus caritas quam potestas, relatum nobis est, quod quidam ex fratribus plus livore odii quam correctionis studio subditos insequentes, dum se simulant spiritalem eis adhibere correctionem, indiscretam subito afferunt mortem, cum inauditos a se proiciunt et illicitis eos iudiciis sub poenitentia puniunt. Non ergo de cetero perversis voluntatibus sit liberum simulare se, quod fingunt, sed quotiescumque quilibet ex subditis corrigendus est, aut publica a sacerdote debet disciplina curari aut, si aliter rectoribus placet, duorum vel trium fratrum spiritalium testimonio peculiariter adhibito et modus criminis agnoscatur et modus poenitentiae irrogetur, ita tamen, ut, si exilio vel retrusione dignum eum esse cognoverit, aut quid aliud iudicium peculiare decreverit, modus poenitentiae, quem coram tribus fratribus sacerdos transgressori indixerit, speciali debeat eius, qui sententiam protulit, manus propriae subscriptione notari. Sicque fiet, ut nec transgressores sine testimonio excidia vitae suae incurrant, nec rectores accusatos se de quorumlibet interemptionibus erubescant."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p18","Canon VIII","Whatever is bestowed by the conferral of invisible grace ought never to be gained or sold for any rewards, the Lord saying: \"What you have received freely, give freely.\" And therefore whoever henceforth, constituted in ecclesiastical order, shall have received any prices or rewards for baptizing or sealing the faithful, or for the chrism, or for promotions of grades, unless voluntarily offered, for this ambition, if indeed such a thing is perpetrated by subjects with the knowledge of the local bishop, let the same bishop be subject to excommunication for two months, because he has concealed evil in knowledge and has not obtained necessary correction; but if any of his own, the same being ignorant, shall have believed that something is to be received by him for the aforesaid chapters, if he is a priest, let him be punished with excommunication for three months; if a deacon, for four; a subdeacon however or a cleric serving these cupidities is to be punished both with competent penalty and with due excommunication.","Quicquid invisibilis gratiae collatione tribuitur, numquam quaesta vel quibuslibet praemiis venundari penitus debet, dicente domino: Quod gratis accepistis, gratis date. Et ideo quicumque deinceps in ecclesiastico ordine constitutus aut pro baptizandis consignandisque fidelibus, chrismatis vel promotionibus graduum pretia quaelibet vel praemia nisi voluntarie oblata pro huiusmodi ambitione susceperit, equidem si sciente loci episcopo tale quicquam a subditis perpetratur, idem episcopus duobus mensibus excommunicationi subiaceat pro eo, quod in scientia mala contexit et correptionem necessariam non adhibuit; sin autem suorum quispiam eodem nesciente quodcumque pro supradictis capitulis accipiendum sibi esse crediderit, si presbyter est, trium mensium excommunicatione plectatur, si diaconus, quattuor, subdiaconus vero vel clericus his cupiditatibus serviens et competenti poena et debita excommunicatione plectendus est."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p19","Canon IX","Many sentences of the fathers have flowed concerning this chapter, namely that no one think the inappreciable grace of the holy Spirit to be purchased by gifts or rewards. But — which is not to be said without grave grief — by as much as this has been prohibited by the frequent precept of decrees, by so much it is known to be repeated by frauds by us, while those who strive to purchase the grace of their ordination by such a price either anticipate the times with a gift or, after the honor received, give by their promises the reward of foul gain to the fathers. And therefore, that the occasion of these and similar arguments may henceforth be cut off, this holy synod has defined that, when anyone about to receive the pontifical summit shall have approached before the altar of the Lord, he be bound by sacraments and by exaction that he has neither given at any time nor in the future may procure to give to any person the collation of any reward for the consecration of honor to be conferred upon himself; and thus either may he receive the consecration of prelation clean from this contagion, or, this crime having been published, may he, manifestly exposed, not approach before the church to the honor which he wished to purchase. Those however whom henceforth it shall have become clear that they were ordained through reward after prelation, we decree that, under defined laws of penance, as truly simoniacs, they are to be separated from the church, that is, that, bound in exile for a space of two years and coerced with worthy satisfaction or by sentence of excommunication, they may endeavor to seek by tears and to repair the grade of honor which they purchased by rewards. Whence if worthy satisfaction shall have found them at the time of penance, they are to be restored not only to communion, but also to the place and to the offices of the whole order from which they had been separated.","Multae super hoc capitulo patrum sententiae manaverunt, scilicet ne inappretiabilem sancti spiritus gratiam donis vel muneribus quis existimet comparandam. Sed quod non sine gravi dolore dicendum est, quanto hoc frequenti decretorum praeceptione prohibitum, tanto a nobis fraudibus cognoscitur iteratum, dum hi, qui tali pretio mercari nituntur gratiam ordinationis suae, tempora praeveniunt munere, aut post acceptum honorem promissis suis conferunt a patribus turpis lucri mercedem. Et ideo, ut horum et similium argumentorum deinceps amputetur occasio, hoc sancta synodus definivit, ut, cum quisque pontificale culmen ante domini altare percepturus accesserit, sacramentis et exactione adstringatur, quod pro conferenda sibi consecratione honoris nulli personae cuiuslibet praemii collationem vel aliquando dedisset vel aliquando in futurum dare procuret, sicque aut mundus ab hoc contagio praelationis consecrationem accipiat, aut publicato hoc scelere manifeste denudatus coram ecclesia ad honorem, quem mercari voluit, non accedat. Illos tamen, quos deinceps post praelationem per praemium ordinatos fuisse patuerit, sub definitis poenitentiae legibus, ut vere simoniacos ab ecclesia separandos esse censemus, id est, ut duorum annorum spatio exilio religati et digna satisfactione vel excommunicationis sententia coerciti honoris gradum, quem praemiis emerant, lacrimis conquirere et reparare intendant. Unde si digna satisfactio eos poenitentiae tempore invenerit, non tantum communioni, sed et loco et totius ordinis officiis, a quibus separati fuerant, restaurandi sunt."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p20","Canon X","Although all who are dedicated to sacred orders are held bound by canonical rules, yet it is expedient that those whom ecclesiastical discipline proves for the grade of promotion should pledge the vows of their promise under caution. For that which is promised singularly is accustomed to be more feared than that which is concluded by a general profession. And therefore it has pleased this holy council, that each one who is about to approach to ecclesiastical grades should not receive the consecration of honor before he promises by the notation of his agreement that, preserving the sincere catholic faith with devotion of heart, he ought to live justly and piously and in none of his works contradict the canonical rules, and that each one render due honor and the reverence of obedience in all things to him who presides over him, according to the edict of the blessed Pope Leo: \"He who knows that he is set over some, let him not bear with resentment that someone is set over him, but the obedience which he demands, let him also himself pay.\" The penalty however according to the custom of ecclesiastical practice is to be inserted into the agreements of such persons and is to be paid by those who shall have been transgressors.","Quamquam omnes, qui sacris mancipantur ordinibus, canonicis regulis teneantur adstricti, expedibile tamen est, ut promissionis suae vota sub cautione spondeant, quos ad promotionis gradus ecclesiastica probat disciplina. Solet enim plus timeri, quod singulariter pollicetur, quam quod generali sponsione concluditur. Et ideo placuit huic sancto concilio, ut unusquisque, qui ad ecclesiasticos gradus est accessurus, non ante honoris consecrationem accipiat, quam placiti sui innotatione promittat, ut fidem catholicam sinceram cordis devotione custodiens iuste et pie vivere debeat et in nullis operibus suis canonicis regulis contradicat atque ut debitum per omnia honorem atque obsequii reverentiam praeminenti sibi unusquisque dependat iuxta illud beati papae Leonis edictum: Qui se scit aliquibus esse praepositum, non moleste ferat aliquem sibi esse praelatum, sed oboedientiam, quam exigit, etiam ipse dependat. Poena tamen iuxta ecclesiasticae consuetudinis morem et placitis talium inserenda et ab his, qui transgressores fuerint, persolvenda est."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p21","Canon XI","Since nothing ought to be confused in the divine canon, nothing doubtful, nothing also indiscreet in the collation of our assembly, a canon of the first Toledan council has been related, in which it is commanded that, if anyone has not taken the Eucharist received from a priest, he is to be driven out as sacrilegious. Nor is the discretion of wills added there, since even an unbeliever confidently receives that which the necessity of nature does not permit him to swallow. For the infirmity of human nature, weighed down at the very end of death, is accustomed to be so depressed by a great weight of dryness that it cannot be refreshed by any taking of foods, but can scarcely be sustained even by the grace of a drink of the same measure. Which we have also seen in the departure of many, who, seeking the viaticum of sacred communion desired by their vows, have rejected the Eucharist bestowed upon them by a priest, not that they did this by unbelief, but because they could not swallow the Eucharist given to them except for the draught of the Lord's cup. Therefore persons of this kind who have done such things not by unbelief but by necessity are not to be separated from the body of the church, especially those concerning whom nothing sinister of faith is perceived. Therefore it has pleased to define that it neither harms a believer nor amounts to much for an unbeliever. Therefore whichever faithful person, compelled by any unavoidable infirmity, has rejected the Eucharist received, let him be subject to no ecclesiastical damnation; similarly nor will the censure of any punishment refute those persons who do such things either at the time of infancy or, placed in any alienation of mind, seem to be ignorant of what they have done. But truly whoever, whether of the number of the faithful or of unbelievers, shall have rejected the body of the Lord without unavoidable infirmity, as has been said, if he is faithful, let him be deprived of perpetual communion; if he is an unbeliever, let him both be subjected to blows and be bound in perpetual exile. But if any such person shall have washed away an excess of this kind with worthy satisfaction of penance, after five years it will be lawful for him to be restored to pristine communion.","Cum nihil in divino canone debeat esse confusum, nihil dubium, nihil etiam indiscretum in collationem nostri coetus, relatus est canon Toletani concilii primi, in quo praeceptum est, ut, si quis acceptam a sacerdote eucharistiam non sumpserit, ut sacrilegus repellatur. Nec adiecta est ibi discretio voluntatum, cum et infidelis fidenter accipiat, quod naturaliter illum necessitas deglutire non sinat. Solet enim humanae naturae infirmitas in ipso mortis exitu praegravata tanto siccitatis pondere deprimi, ut nullis ciborum illationibus refici, sed vix tantundem illati delectetur poculi gratia sustentari. Quod etiam in multorum exitu vidimus, qui optatum suis votis sacrae communionis expetentes viaticum collatam sibi a sacerdote eucharistiam reiecerunt, non quod infidelitate hoc agerent, sed quod praeter dominici calicis haustum traditam sibi non possent eucharistiam deglutire. Non ergo huiusmodi a corpore ecclesiae separandi sunt, qui talia non infidelitate, sed necessitate fecerunt, praesertim hi, de quibus nihil fidei sinistrae sentitur. Placuit ergo definire, quod nec fideli officiat nec infideli multum existat. Quicumque ergo fidelis inevitabili qualibet infirmitate coactus eucharistiam perceptam reiecerit, in nullo ecclesiasticae damnationi subiaceat; similiter nec illos cuiusquam punitionis censura redarguet, qui talia aut tempore infantiae faciunt aut in quolibet mentis alienatione positi, quid fecerint, ignorare videntur. Iam vero quicumque aut de fidelium aut de infidelium numero corpus domini absque inevitabili, ut dictum est, infirmitate proiecerit, si fidelis est, perpetua communione privetur; si infidelis est, et verberibus subdatur et perpetuo exilio religetur. Quod si horum quislibet huiusmodi excessus digna poenitentiae satisfactione defleverit, post quinquennium licitum erit illum communioni pristinae reformari."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p22","Canon XII","He who does penance in the peril of death is not to be long deferred from the grace of reconciliation, but if the peril of death urges him being girded, penance having been received through the imposition of hands, reconciliation is to be applied immediately, lest the sick man depart from human affairs before he receives the gift of reconciliation, and thus to the survivors it might seem of perpetual grief, if the nature of death has snatched away prematurely from the present life him who did not deserve reconciliation. Therefore according to the edict of Pope Leo, those who in a time of necessity and in the pressing instance of peril implore the help of penance with the custom of reconciliation, neither is satisfaction to be forbidden nor is reconciliation to be denied, because in the mercy of God we can neither set measures nor define times. Concerning those however who, having received penance, have departed from this life before they are reconciled, although there is a diversity of precepts concerning this chapter, yet that sentence has pleased us which, with a manifold number, has decreed more humanely concerning such matters, that both the memory of such persons be commended in the churches and the oblation dedicated for them be received for spiritual things.","Qui poenitentiam in mortis agit periculo, non diutine a reconciliationis gratia differendus est, sed si praecincto mortis urguetur periculum, poenitentia per manus impositionem accepta, statim et reconciliatio adhibenda est, ne prius ab humanis rebus aeger abscedat, quam donum reconciliationis accipiat, sicque superstitibus quodammodo doloris videatur esse perpetui, si praecisum ab ecclesiae membris eum, qui utique reconciliationem non meruit, raptim a praesenti vita mortis natura subduxerit. Unde iuxta papae Leonis edictum hi, qui in tempore necessitatis et in periculi urgentis instantia praesidium poenitentiae ex more reconciliationis implorant, nec satisfactio interdicenda est nec reconciliatio deneganda, quia in misericordia dei nec mensuras possumus ponere nec tempora definire. De his autem, qui acceptam poenitentiam, antequam reconcilientur, ab hac vita recesserint, quamquam diversitas praeceptorum de hoc capitulo habeatur, illorum tamen nobis sententiam placuit, qui multiplici numero de huiusmodi humanius decreverunt, ut et memoria talium in ecclesiis commendetur et oblatio pro eorum dedicata spiritalibus accipiatur."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p23","Canon XIII","Indeed it has been well defined by the rules of the greater ones that it is not lawful for those entangled with demons and other similar passions to handle the sacred mysteries. To which precept, the assent of reason having been applied, we have commonly defined this: that none of those who either, seized by demons, fall to the ground, or are carried away by the incursions of any vexation, should dare to minister at the sacred altars or thrust themselves into the divine sacraments without being examined, except those who, given to various bodily comforts, are proved to fall to the ground without such passions; who yet, they themselves, are to be suspended from the order and place of their office for so long until, for a space of one year, by the discretion of the bishop, they are found to be free from the incursion of demons.","Bene siquidem maiorum regulis definitum est, ut daemoniis aliisque similibus passionibus irretitis mysteria sacra tractare non liceat. Cui praecepto consensu rationis adhibito, id communiter definivimus, ut nullus de his, qui aut in terram arrepti a daemonibus eliduntur aut quolibet modo vexationis incursibus efferuntur, vel sacris audeant ministrare altariis vel indiscussi se divinis ingerant sacramentis, exceptis illis, qui variis corporum commoditatibus dediti sine huiusmodi passionibus in terram approbantur elisi; qui tamen et ipsi tamdiu erunt ab officii sui ordine et loco suspensi, quousque unius anni spatio per discretionem episcopi inveniuntur ab incursu daemonum alieni."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p24","Canon XIV","That divine oracle, which warns each one taking care, by which it is written: \"Woe to the solitary, because when he falls, he has no one to lift him up,\" is greatly to be feared and guarded by us, lest at those hours and times at which psalm-singing is done to the Lord or is sacrificed, there occur to anyone singly attending to divine offices a pernicious passion or any bodily illness, which either suddenly prostrates the body or confuses the mind by alienation or terror. Therefore, taking care against such hazards, we have deemed it necessary to establish that where the abundance of time or place or clergy suffices, whoever that person is who sings to God and sacrifices should have a helper of neighboring solace behind him, so that if by any chance he who approaches to fulfill the offices shall have been disturbed or cast down to the ground, he may always have from behind someone who may carry out his role intrepidly.","Ut illud divini oraculi monentis singuli praecaventes, quo scribitur: Vae soli, quia, cum ceciderit, non habet sublevantem, summopere verendum nobis est et cavendum, ne horis illis atque temporibus, quibus domino psallitur vel sacrificatur, unicuique divinis singulariter officiis insistenti perniciosa passio vel corporis quaelibet valitudo occurrat, quae aut corpus subito subrui faciat aut mentem alienatione vel terrore confundat. Pro huiusmodi ergo casibus praecaventes necessarium duximus instituere, ut ubi temporis vel loci sive cleri copia suffragatur, habeat quisquis ille canens deo atque sacrificans post se vicini solaminis adiutorem, ut, si aliquo casu ille, qui officia impleturus accedit, turbatus fuerit vel ad terram elisus, a tergo semper habeat, qui eius vicem exsequatur intrepidus."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p25","Canon XV","All things having been completed which have been promulgated in this council for the correction of our order, it has pleased to define that, obeying the paternal institutions, every year we assemble with always devoted studies of minds in the metropolitan see for performing the celebration of the council at the time which the election of the prince or of the metropolitan shall have defined, nor absent ourselves on any required occasions, but on the prepared day on which it shall have been appointed, all the pontiffs of the province having been assembled in the metropolitan see, let the council be celebrated with God presiding. But if any of the bishops, excepted by an unavoidable cause or necessity, shall have absented himself from performing the council, let him be punished with excommunication for one year. But if henceforth without the celebration of the council he shall have passed the limits of one year, he shall be held obnoxious to the sentence of the superior censure of all the common pontiffs of the Carthaginian province, that is, if no power of the prince, or infirmity, or unavoidable cause impedes him, but they have not gathered themselves for celebrating the council solely by the will of their own inclination.","Peractis omnibus, quae ad correctionem nostri ordinis in hoc concilio promulgata sunt, placuit definire, ut paternis institutionibus obsequentes omni anno ad peragendam celebritatem concilii in metropolitana sede tempore, quo principis vel metropolitani electio definierit, devotis semper animorum studiis conferamus, nec quibuslibet requisitis occasionibus absentemur, sed in praeparato die, quo indictum fuerit, adunatis in metropolitana sede omnibus provinciae pontificibus concilium deo praesule celebretur. Quisquis autem episcoporum excepto inevitabili causa vel necessitate de peragendo se concilio absentaverit, unius anni metas erit excommunicatione plectendus. Quod si deinceps absque celebratione concilii anni unius metas transierit, omnium in commune pontificum Carthaginis provinciae superioris censurae sententiam obnoxius retinebit, id est, si nulla sibi impediente principis potestate vel infirmitate aut inevitabili causa, sed solius propriae voluntatis libitu sese ad celebrandum concilium non collegerint."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p26","Canon XVI","Therefore to these our constitutions, which we believed were necessarily to be decreed, we have appended the final subscription of our hand, rendering immortal glory and honor to our God and Lord, who has made us joyful from the assembly of mutual vision, who has opened our mouth in the confession of His praise, who also has honorably completed the decrees of our council. After these things we give thanks to the religious lord and our amiable prince, King Wamba, by whose study also we have been gathered together, who, appearing as a new restorer of ecclesiastical discipline in our times, not only intended to restore the omitted orders of councils, but also established them to be celebrated in annual recurrences, so that for mutual correction of morals, eagerly running together at the annual time, according to the prophecy of the prophet, that which is broken among us may be bound together, and that which has been cast away may be brought back. May the Lord therefore give to the same prince, on account of the vow of this sacred solicitude, both to pass the course of this present life in peace and, after long times, iniquities having been remitted, to come to Himself in peace, so that both he may lead happy times, and happy with all over whom he presides, he may come to Christ without confusion, so that, because through him the crown of our order is restored for the better, he may receive the crown of the future kingdom from this in the region of the living, reigning with Christ for ever and ever. Amen.","His igitur constitutionibus nostris, quas necessario decernendas credidimus, finalem manus nostrae subscriptionem adiecimus, immortali deo nostro et domino gloriam et honorem reddentes, qui nos de conventu alternae visionis laetos efficit, qui os nostrum in confessione laudis suae aperuit, qui etiam decreta huius nostri concilii honeste complevit. Post haec religioso domino et amabili principi nostro Wambani regi gratiarum actiones persolvimus, cuius etiam studio aggregati sumus, qui ecclesiasticae disciplinae nostris saeculis novus reparator occurrens omissos conciliorum ordines non solum restaurare intendit, sed etiam annuis recursibus celebrandos instituit, ut ad alternam morum correctionem annuo tempore alacriter concurrentes iuxta prophetae vaticinium, quod in nobis defractum est, alligetur, et quod abiectum est, reducatur. Det ergo eidem principi dominus pro huius sacrae sollicitudinis voto et cursum praesentis vitae in pace transire et post diuturna tempora ad se in pace remissis iniquitatibus pervenire, qualiter et ille felicia tempora ducat, et felix cum omnibus, quibus principatur, ad Christum sine confusione perveniat, ut, quia per eum corona nostri ordinis in melius restauretur, coronam futuri regni capiat ex hoc in regione vivorum regnans cum Christo in saecula saeculorum. Amen."],["vol1/toledo-xi/index.html","p27","Canon XVI","There were present at this council seventeen pontiffs. I, Quiricus, metropolitan bishop of the royal city, have subscribed these synodal acts defined by us. Similarly also the other bishops subscribed, sixteen in number.","Interfuerunt huic concilio pontifices XVII. Ego Quiricus, urbis regiae metropolitanus episcopus, haec gesta synodica a nobis definita subscripsi. Similiter et alii episcopi subscripserunt numero XVI."]];